p('Figure 1  Chen notation ER diagram showing all six core tables and all 37 column names. Rectangles represent entities, ovals represent attributes and diamonds represent relationships. Underlined attributes identify primary keys; FK and UQ annotations identify foreign and unique keys.')
h('Reading the relationships')
p('Users make bookings; stations contain chargers; chargers are booked for reservations. Each booking may generate at most one charging session, and each session may have at most one payment record. Every child references exactly one parent. The testing log is an auxiliary table outside this core business model.')
page('04  /  DATABASE DESIGN','Integrity and normalization')
table(['Relation','Candidate keys','Foreign key target'],[['users','user_id; email','None'],['stations','station_id','None'],['chargers','charger_id; (station_id, charger_code)','station_id → stations'],['bookings','booking_id','user_id → users; charger_id → chargers'],['charging_sessions','session_id; booking_id','booking_id → bookings'],['payments','payment_id; session_id','session_id → charging_sessions']])
h('Domains and constraints')
p('Every primary key is an auto increment integer. All five foreign keys use ON DELETE RESTRICT and ON UPDATE CASCADE. UNIQUE booking_id permits at most one session per booking; UNIQUE session_id permits at most one payment record per session. Parent records may exist without children.')
p('CHECK constraints require positive charger power, nonnegative rates and amounts, a booking end after its start, and valid optional session times and energy. Email uses a simple LIKE shape check. Session end and energy may be NULL while charging is in progress. Payment method and paid_at may be NULL before settlement. Complete data types and enum domains appear in the companion table creation script.')
h('From repeating records to 3NF')
table(['Form','Design principle','Project interpretation'],[['1NF','One scalar value per attribute','Separate rows for bookings and sessions'],['2NF','No partial dependency on a candidate key','Charger facts depend on its full identity'],['3NF','No nonkey transitive dependency','Station details reside in stations; user details in users']])
p('A hypothetical combined booking log would repeat user names and station addresses. UserID → user details and StationID → station details justify separating these facts. The actual schema is consistent with 3NF under its modeled dependencies; a surrogate primary key alone does not prove normalization. City names are not assumed to determine a unique state.')
h('Why store the session rate')
p('chargers.rate_per_kwh is the current catalog rate. charging_sessions.rate_per_kwh is the captured historical price. These have different meanings. The invoice uses ROUND(energy_kwh × rate_per_kwh, 2). However, direct SQL can still edit the snapshot or invoice: the implementation does not make them immutable.')
